course: everyday
lesson: 100
title: Standing up for yourself, nightmares and the dark
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Standing up for yourself and others works best with calm, clear words: «Stop, that's mine», «I was here first», «I don't think that joke is funny»; pushing back makes things worse, and staying silent can look like agreement.
explain: You can even disagree with an adult, if you are polite and explain your reason calmly; respectful words make adults listen.
explain: Be kind to yourself too: pictures online show only people's best moments, and bodies come in many shapes; looking after yours with good food, exercise and sleep matters more than comparing.
explain: Nightmares can feel very real, but they are only dreams and cannot hurt you; turning on a light, going to a parent, calm music and imagining a better ending all help, and monsters under the bed are not real.
explain: A girl in Christchurch has the same dream about a wolf every week; she draws it, then draws the wolf turning into a friendly dog that walks her home, and the nightmare stops coming back.
fact: If someone pushes in front of you, politely say you were there first or tell an adult.
fact: Calm words work better than pushing back.
fact: If someone keeps taking your things, say clearly «Stop, that's mine» and tell an adult.
fact: Staying silent about an unkind joke about a group of people can seem like agreement.
fact: You can disagree with an adult if you are polite and explain your reason calmly.
fact: Pictures online show only people's best moments.
fact: Bodies come in many shapes, and all deserve care with good food, exercise and sleep.
fact: Dreams can feel real, but they cannot hurt you.
fact: Drawing a repeated nightmare and imagining a better ending can stop it coming back.
fact: Cuddling a toy, calm music or reading helps the body relax after a bad dream.
fact: Monsters are not real, and looking with a torch and a parent shows there is nothing there.
fact: Turning on lights as you go makes the dark less scary.
exercise: Someone pushes in front of you in the lunch queue. What should you do? | Politely say you were there first, or tell an adult | Push them back out of the queue | Shout at them in front of everyone | Say nothing at all and stay angry with them for the rest of the day
exercise: Why are calm words better than pushing back? | They work better and do not start a fight | They make the other person cry | They make you look weak and quiet | They always get the other person punished by a teacher
exercise: A boy keeps taking your rubber without asking. What should you say? | Stop, that's mine, and then tell an adult if he keeps on | Nothing, and buy another rubber | Take his rubber so it is even | Tell him you will never be his friend again
exercise: Someone makes an unkind joke about people from another country. What can you do? | Say you don't think it's funny | Laugh along so you do not seem rude | Stay silent and walk away from them | Make a joke about their country back
exercise: Why does it matter to speak up about an unkind joke? | Staying silent can seem like agreement | Speaking up always makes you popular | Jokes are never allowed at school | The joker will surely say sorry
exercise: You disagree with your coach about your position on the team. What should you do? | Explain your reason politely and calmly | Refuse to play until you get your own way | Complain to your teammates about the coach | Say nothing and stop trying in any games
exercise: Mia feels bad because her friends online look perfect. What is true? | Online pictures show only their best moments | Everyone else's life really is perfect | She should post only perfect-looking pictures of herself too | She must be the only one with bad days
exercise: A boy feels bad about his body. What helps? | Look after it with food, exercise and sleep, and talk to a trusted adult | Stop eating proper meals so that his body changes shape faster | Compare himself with people online every day | Hide his feelings and never talk about them
exercise: You wake up from a scary nightmare. What helps? | Remind yourself it was a dream, turn on a light, go to a parent if needed | Stay frozen under the covers until morning | Keep thinking about the dream over and over | Get up and play games until it is light
exercise: Why can a nightmare not hurt you? | It is only a dream made by your sleeping brain | It can hurt you, but only if you scream out loud in your sleep | Nightmares only hurt people who are awake | The monster is real but far away
exercise: You keep having the same nightmare. What could help? | Tell a parent, draw it and imagine a better ending | Stop sleeping so the dream cannot come | Watch more scary films to get used to it | Never tell anyone about the dream
exercise: After a nightmare you are scared to go back to sleep. What helps you relax? | Cuddle a toy, listen to calm music or read | Play an exciting video game | Eat a big sugary snack | Think about the scariest part again and again
exercise: You think there is a monster under the bed. What can you do? | Look with a torch together with a parent | Never get out of bed at night ever again | Throw things under the bed to scare it off | Sleep on the floor so it cannot reach you
exercise: You are scared to go downstairs alone at night. What helps? | Turn on lights as you go or ask someone to come | Run down in the dark as fast as you can | Close your eyes tight all the way down | Stay upstairs even if you need the toilet
test: A girl in Toronto has her pencil case taken every day by a classmate. Which response is clear but calm? | Saying «Stop, that's mine» and telling the teacher | Grabbing it back hard and pushing him away from her desk | Taking his bag in return | Crying and saying nothing
test: At a party a boy makes fun of people who wear glasses. Most children laugh. What can one child do? | Say it is not funny | Laugh quietly so he fits in | Take his glasses off to hide them | Make a joke about the boy's clothes
test: A boy in Kent thinks his teacher marked his answer wrong by mistake. What is the best way to say so? | Politely ask and show the teacher his reasoning | Shout that the teacher is wrong | Tell his parents to complain at once | Just accept it and stay angry
test: Lily sees a friend's holiday photos and feels her own life is boring. What is helpful to remember? | People share their best moments, not their dull ones | Her friend's life is perfect every day | She should feel jealous of her friend and stop talking to her | She must go on a holiday to be happy
test: Which is the healthiest way to feel good about your body? | Look after it with food, sleep and exercise | Compare it every day with the bodies in adverts | Skip meals so that its shape changes quickly | Hide it under big baggy clothes all the time
test: A boy in Arizona wakes from a dream about a snake in his bed. What is the sensible first step? | Remember it was a dream and turn on the light | Jump out and run outside | Stay awake for the rest of the night just in case | Shake his blanket out of the window
test: Why does imagining a better ending to a nightmare help? | Changing the ending can stop the dream coming back | It makes you stay asleep all night long without waking up | It gives the monster a reason to leave | It teaches you to stop dreaming
test: A little brother is sure a monster lives in his wardrobe. What will most likely help? | Opening it together with a torch to show it is empty | Telling him to stop being a baby and just go to sleep now | Locking the wardrobe so the monster cannot get out | Telling him the monster only comes out at midnight
test: A girl needs a drink of water at night but the hallway is dark. What is best? | Switch on the lights as she goes | Walk down with her eyes shut tight | Wait until morning without a drink | Run through the dark as fast as she can
test: kid pushed in front of me in the lunch line | Politely say you were there first or tell an adult | Push him back out of the line so he learns his lesson | Shove in front of someone else | Throw your tray at him
test: he keeps nicking my pencils every day | Say clearly "Stop, that's mine" and tell an adult | Take his pencils back twice so it's fair | Hide all your things forever | Say nothing and buy more
test: had the same scary dream 3 nights now 😢 | Draw it and imagine a better ending | Stay awake every single night from now on | Watch a scary film to get used to it | Never tell anyone at all about the dream
test: theres a monster under my bed i heard it | Monsters are not real, look with a torch and a parent | Yes, keep your feet under the covers all night so it can't grab u | Sleep on the floor instead | Shout at it so it goes away
explain: A boy in Ohio watched a kid being pushed around every day and said nothing; one day the kid was hurt badly, and the boy wished he had told a teacher sooner.
explain: A girl in Wales watched a scary film late at night, then could not sleep for a week and was too tired to learn.
fact: Telling an adult when you see someone being hurt is standing up for them, and it is not telling tales.
fact: Scary films and videos before bed often cause nightmares and bad sleep in children.
test: kid in my class gets pushed round every day, not my business | Tell a teacher, standing up helps stop it | Stay out of it, it's not your problem, it's theirs | Join in so they leave you alone | Film it for a laugh
test: watched a horror film at a sleepover, now can't sleep | Tell a parent, and next time pick a film for your age | Watch another one straight away to get used to it | Never sleep with the lights off again | Stay up all night instead
test: having the same nightmare every night for ages | Tell a parent, talking about it can help | Never go to bed | Keep it secret, it's babyish to tell anyone about dreams | Eat a big meal right before bed
test: Ava's telling everyone some boy watched a horror film at a sleepover n had nightmares so bad he never slept again, like ever. what's probably exaggerated | Never sleeping again; scary films cause bad dreams, not endless waking | The part about the sleepover, because those never happen | That scary films can give anyone nightmares at all | Nothing, since a nightmare really can stop a person ever sleeping again
test: heard a kid got pushed around every day by the lockers n nobody said anything, now he's hurt, people say the ones who watched are just as bad. what's the real lesson | Telling an adult when someone is hurt is standing up, not telling tales | Watching quietly is the safest choice for everybody | Only teachers should notice when a kid is being hurt, it is their job not ours | Pushing the bully back is the best way to help
test: my brother says Zoe still sleeps with the light on n a teddy, in year 5 lol, should i tell my mates | No, it is private and unkind to spread, and lights help many people | Yes, because it is funny that she is still scared of the dark at her age | Yes, so she learns to stop being a baby | Yes, but only to friends who already know her
exam: Identify the principal reason for which calm and clear words are preferred to physical retaliation when one's possessions are repeatedly taken. | Calm words are more effective, whereas retaliation aggravates matters | Retaliation is preferred, as it concludes the matter at once and for good | Calm words oblige the other party to pay compensation | Physical retaliation is permitted on a second occasion only
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a nightmare? | It may feel real, but it is a dream and cannot inflict harm | It is a warning of events that will subsequently occur in the dreamer's life | It is caused by monsters present within the bedroom | It may inflict harm if the dreamer does not awaken
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning disagreement with an adult. | It is permissible if expressed politely with a reason given | It is in all circumstances forbidden to a child | It is permissible only if expressed loudly and firmly | It is permissible only in the presence of another adult who agrees
exam: Ascertain the course of action most likely to prevent the recurrence of a repeated nightmare. | Drawing the dream and imagining a more agreeable ending | Remaining awake throughout the night to avoid dreaming | Viewing a frightening film immediately before retiring to bed | Declining to mention the dream to any person at all
